Folate Deficiency Anaemia
Haematology & Oncology
Profound fatigue/pallor, Smooth beefy red tongue, Absence of neurological deficits, Macrocytic anaemia with low serum folate

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-020
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of profound weakness and recurrent syncope upon standing, requiring urgent evaluation for severe symptomatic anaemia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly patient with poor dietary intake experiencing sudden, recurrent fainting spells whenever they attempted to stand, accompanied by agonizing oral pain. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to rule out acute cardiovascular collapse or severe anaemia causing orthostatic instability, which posed a high fall risk if left unassessed overnight.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I feel completely drained of all energy, my mouth is so sore I can barely eat, and I passed out twice today when trying to get out of my chair."
History of Present Illness: The 72-year-old patient reports an insidious onset of generalized fatigue and severe systemic lethargy progressing over the last 3 months. Over the past 48 hours, the weakness escalated sharply, culminating in two episodes of syncope upon standing today. They complain of a smooth, "beefy red," and exquisitely sore tongue (glossitis), along with frequent recurrent aphthous oral ulcers that have drastically reduced their already poor dietary intake (consisting mostly of tea and toast, completely lacking fresh green leafy vegetables). They specifically deny any tingling or numbness in the hands or feet.
Functional Status: Bedbound/chairbound due to orthostatic syncope and profound exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of traumatic injury from recurrent falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 (Supine) -> 85/50 (Standing - Positive Orthostatic Drop) | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Frail, pale, and lethargic elderly adult. Appears chronically malnourished.
Physical Exam:
HEENT: Marked pallor of the conjunctivae and oral mucous membranes. The tongue is visibly smooth, inflamed, and beefy red (glossitis) with angular stomatitis present at the corners of the mouth. Mild scleral icterus is noted.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Soft systolic flow murmur auscultated.
Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetrical. Sensation to light touch, pinprick, and vibration is completely intact distally in all four limbs (absolute absence of neurological deficits). Romberg is negative when supported.
Extremities: Marked pallor of the palmar creases. No oedema.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen reveals only highly processed, shelf-stable foods; no fresh produce.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent changes to medications. No antihypertensives that would cause the orthostatic drop.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Folate Deficiency Anaemia - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Orthostatic Hypotension)
Differentials:

Vitamin B12 Deficiency (Pernicious Anaemia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the complete absence of peripheral neuropathy, dorsal column signs (loss of vibration sense), or other neurological deficits that typically accompany severe B12 deficiency.

Acute Gastrointestinal Bleed - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the chronic, insidious timeline, lack of melena/haematemesis, and the classic presence of severe glossitis pointing directly to a nutritional macrocytic anaemia.

Severe Dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the profound chronicity of the fatigue, the classic beefy red tongue, and the scleral icterus (indicating ineffective erythropoiesis) rather than isolated volume depletion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits severe orthostatic hypotension and recurrent syncope driven by profound symptomatic anaemia. The classic physical signs of a beefy red tongue, angular stomatitis, and poor dietary history strongly indicate a severe nutritional deficiency. The complete absence of neurological deficits distinctly points toward Folate Deficiency rather than B12 deficiency. Delaying assessment until daytime hours would subject this frail, malnourished patient to repeated syncopal falls, resulting in likely traumatic fractures or head injuries. The urgent home visit was critical to physically recognize the severe orthostatic instability, diagnose the likely haematological cause, and institute strict safety measures prior to urgent phlebotomy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised strict bed rest. Urgent outpatient pathology ordered for tomorrow morning: FBC (anticipating macrocytic anaemia with elevated MCV and hypersegmented neutrophils), serum/RBC folate, Vitamin B12, and iron studies. Prescribed a high-dose oral folic acid supplement to commence immediately after the blood draw tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Transition from lying to sitting incredibly slowly, and do not attempt to walk unassisted tonight. By allowing your cardiovascular system time to adjust to gravity, you prevent the blood from draining from your brain, eliminating the immediate risk of fainting.

Consume a liquid nutritional supplement or smooth, cold foods tonight. By avoiding hot, acidic, or rough foods, you reduce the mechanical pain on your inflamed tongue, allowing you to take in necessary calories safely.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to "Meals on Wheels" and a community dietitian.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode strictly recommended for tonight.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to review pathology and ensure oral folate therapy is tolerated.

